		<label>MAT-49 7.62</label>
		<description>Caliber: 7.62x25mm Tokarev. 35-round detachable mag. Select-Fire.

The MAT-49 is a French submachine gun designed in the 1940s as part of the extensive rearmament campaign performed by France in the aftermath of World War II. Widely used by all forms of the French military, the MAT-49 was fairly cheap to mass produce and quite reliable in its performance, giving it 24 straight years of production before it was finally discontinued in 1973. The weapon has some unique quirks, including the ability to fold its magazine well/front grip to make it more compact for carry.

MAT-49s were frequently captured by the Viet Minh during the French-Indochina War. In the aftermath of that conflict, the PAVN converted large numbers of stored MAT-49s to 7.62x25mm Tokarev as ammunition in that caliber was supplied frequently by the USSR and China. This is one such example of a 7.62 conversion.</description>
